Predict negative activations for n-fragments following consonants.
Vowels, another n, word boundaries, longer fragments, and title case
usually weaken the response. Coefficients summarize training examples.
Related Arabic, Greek, Japanese and Korean n-spellings have
script-specific rules. Short space-prefixed fragments and hyphenated
n-fragments are handled separately. A Markdown hash at document start is
an independent negative trigger. The program retains just the last
character and document label, not a dictionary of token identities.
"""
import unicodedata

READS = ()
LOW_GAIN_VOWELS = set('aeiаеоияэ')
MEDIUM_GAIN_VOWELS = set('ouуёыю')
HIGH_GAIN_CONSONANTS = set('bpmfgvwhcбпмфг')
CODE_WORDS = {'name', 'new', 'num', 'null'}


def base_letter(char):
    return unicodedata.normalize('NFD', char.lower())[0] if char else ''


